  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nA fairly typical and benign summer-time setup can be expected the \nfirst full week of August as South Texas lies on the eastern flank \nof mid-lvl ridging over the desert SW. At the lower-lvls S-SE flow \nwill gradually increase mid to late week as the subtropical highs \nbuilds westward into the Gulf. Outside of localized moisture pooling \nalong the sea-breeze, ambient PWATs will generally run 1.4 to 1.7 \ninches through the period (generally 25th to 50th percentile for \nclimo). This setup will maintain largely dry conditions with any \nstorm chances confined to the sea breeze. Rain chances will \ngenerally be 10 percent or less (but not entirely zero) through the \nearly week period, before likely creeping up somewhat (mainly over \nthe Victoria Crossroads) late week as low-lvl southerly flow \nincreases. \n\nAs mentioned in previous discussions we will remain seasonably warm \nthrough the forecast period with high temperatures largely in the \ntriple digits west of I-77, and 90s elsewhere.\nThese temperatures combined with lows barely clearing 80 degrees \nwill maintain a moderate to major heat risk each day. Max heat \nindices will also flirt with heat advisory criteria each day in the \nmid-week period and short-fused advisories will need to be \nconsidered. Finally, for anyone doing strenuous activities in direct \nsunlight, WBGT values will peak in the high to extreme range, \noccasionally even exceeding 90 degrees. To sum it all up, make sure \nto stay hydrated, take frequent cooling breaks, and seek shade when \npossible as the dog days of summer are upon us in South Texas.",
